§2 THE RULE AS F.3 STATES IT — two forms, printed one after the other, and they do not have one extent
   RULE-H (headline, L11295): "**A constraint refuted by its own extent is dead.** This is F.3's rule and it is the fourth column"
   RULE-E (elaboration, L11296-L11298): 'turned on the constraint itself. If a constraint is asserted over a ground, and computing its extent on that ground returns the empty set or the whole of it, the constraint has been refuted by the only test that could reach it — not weakened, not pending, dead. The work applies this without exceptio'
   RULE-H is the bare refutation sentence                         True                     OK
   RULE-E names the mechanism: empty set or the whole of it       True                     OK
   RULE-E names the consequence: not weakened, not pending, dead  True                     OK
   CONVENTION RULE-E: a firing is a constraint asserted over a ground whose extent COMPUTED ON THAT GROUND
   is the empty set or the whole of it, the constraint being consequently dead. CONVENTION RULE-H: a firing
   is a constraint killed by the computation of its own extent, by whatever shape that computation takes.
   RULE-H contains RULE-E. Both are scored, because F.3 prints both and the book cites the headline.
   CONVENTION OBJ-C / OBJ-T: F.3 says *constraint*. §4 names a separate mechanism, *a test that could not
   fail* (§28.7.9 L7691), and the Register works it as register 784's pattern. OBJ-C counts only constraints,
   bounds, laws, criteria and clauses asserted over a ground; OBJ-T adds the checks and tests of that class.

